│ patch_description        │ The current approach uses 8 Dirichlet restarts, 30,000 annealing iterations each, an      │
│                          │ interior-biased Dirichlet concentration of 1.15, soft-min scale cooling from 0.0100 to    │
│                          │ 0.00065, and directional local polishing. Its main limitation is that the final local     │
│                          │ search only tests a finite collection of directions.                                      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This version uses a more diverse deterministic initialization portfolio:                  │
│                          │ low-concentration boundary-seeking Dirichlet samples, moderate interior samples, and      │
│                          │ jittered structured lattice templates. Annealing is shortened to 14,000 iterations per    │
│                          │ restart but uses 14 restarts and includes occasional two-point perturbations to escape    │
│                          │ poor oriented-matroid cells. The principal new refinement is an exact coordinate-wise     │
│                          │ maximin linear-program step implemented by half-plane clipping in the simplex. With all   │
│                          │ other points fixed, every signed triangle determinant involving one movable point is      │
│                          │ affine, so this step finds the best feasible location for that point within its current   │
│                          │ orientation cell rather than merely probing several directions. This produces             │
│                          │ substantially stronger final balancing of tight triangle constraints while retaining      │
│                          │ deterministic behavior and graceful finite-value checks.                                  │
