│ patch_description        │ The previous implementation used 14 sequential annealing restarts × 14,000 iterations,    │
│                          │ with relatively expensive Python-level candidate evaluation and only five LP-refined      │
│                          │ basins. Its key settings were a single-point Gaussian move schedule (`0.125 → 0.00065`),  │
│                          │ tail-merit weight `0.055`, 14 restarts, and 22 coordinate-LP sweeps.                      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This replacement uses a vectorized population annealer: 48 simultaneous deterministic     │
│                          │ candidate configurations, 5,600 annealing generations, danger-triangle-weighted point     │
│                          │ selection, adaptive cooling, periodic elite recombination, and several                    │
│                          │ structured/Dirichlet initializations. Evaluating all 48 candidates in one NumPy tensor    │
│                          │ operation substantially reduces Python overhead while exploring more independent          │
│                          │ combinatorial cells. It retains exact coordinate maximin LP polishing, but applies it to  │
│                          │ a larger and more diverse elite set. The lower-tail merit is strengthened during global   │
│                          │ search, while final selection remains strictly based on the true minimum normalized area. │
